<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Montmédy</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Montmédy is a small town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, near the Belgian border, dominated by a fortified citadel on a hill. Founded by the counts of Chiny in the 13th century, it later belonged to Luxembourg and the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> until France took it in 1657, after which Vauban strengthened its defences. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> was trying to reach it when he was stopped at Varennes in 1791.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/montmedy.jpg" alt="Montmédy" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Pol GRANDMONT · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Montm%C3%A9dy_haut_Fo1JPG.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
